      "text": "To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 12 and 20, we can use the Euclidean algorithm. The Euclidean algorithm is an efficient method for computing the greatest common divisor of two numbers, which is based on the principle that the GCD of two numbers also divides their difference.\n\nHere are the steps:\n\n1. Divide 20 by 12 and find the remainder.\n   \\[\n   20 = 12 \\cdot 1 + 8\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is 8.\n\n2. Now, replace 20 with 12 and 12 with 8, and repeat the process.\n   \\[\n   12 = 8 \\cdot 1 + 4\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is 4.\n\n3. Replace 12 with 8 and 8 with 4, and repeat the process.\n   \\[\n   8 = 4 \\cdot 2 + 0\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is 0.\n\nWhen the remainder becomes 0, the divisor at that step is the GCD. Therefore, the GCD of 12 and 20 is 4.\n\nThus, the greatest common divisor of 12 and 20 is \\boxed{4}.",
